'''Trans Maldivian Airways (Pvt) Ltd.''' (TMA) is a private [[airline]] headquartered on the grounds of [[Velana International Airport]] in [[Malé]], [[Maldives]].<ref>"[http://www.transmaldivian.com/contact/ Contact]." Trans Maldivian Airways. Retrieved on April 21, 2015. "TRANS MALDIVIAN AIRWAYS (Pvt) Ltd. Ibrahim Nasir International Airport P.O. Box 2079 Male’ Republic of Maldives"</ref>

Operating out of [[Velana International Airport]], TMA is the oldest air transfer operator operating in the country, providing seaplane transfer services to a large number of tourist resorts. TMA in 2013 operated the world's largest seaplane fleet.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url = http://www.blackstone.com/news-views/press-releases/details/blackstone-announces-acquisition-of-a-majority-stake-in-maldivian-air-taxi-and-trans-maldivian-airways|title = Blackstone Press Release|date = 3 February 2013|accessdate = 11 December 2014|website = Blackstone Announces Acquisition of a Majority Stake in Maldivian Air Taxi and Trans Maldivian Airways|publisher = |last = |first = }}</ref> As of 2016, December it operates out of Gan International Airport, servicing resorts in Addu, and Huvadhu Atoll.

== History ==

=== Hummingbird Island Airways ===
The airline was founded in 1989 as Hummingbird Island Helicopters by pilot Kit Chambers. The company operated a strictly helicopter fleet of aircraft, moving tourists from the airport to a select number of island resorts. 1993 saw the establishment of [[Maldivian Air Taxi]], a direct competitor offering more-preferred seaplane transfers. In order to revive the market, the company was rebranded as Hummingbird Island Airways in 1997, introducing Twin Otter seaplanes to its fleet. By 1999, the fleet had transitioned out all helicopters and had a seaplane-only lineup.

=== Trans Maldivian Airways ===
In the year 2000, Hummingbird Island was rebranded as Trans Maldivian Airways, under new management. In the next years, TMA acquired a fleet of 16 [[Twin Otter]] seaplanes, operating alongside [[Maldivian Air Taxi]] to provide transfer services to a growing number of tourist resorts in numerous atolls of the archipelago.

In 2006, TMA announced intentions to acquire 3 [[ATR 42]] aircraft to begin operations to the domestic airports scattered in the atolls.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.haveeru.com.mv/dhivehi/business/38485|title = TMA to acquire three 46-seat aircraft to fly to internal airports of Maldives. (Dhivehi)|date = 7 October 2006|accessdate = 11 December 2014|website = Haveeru Online|publisher = Haveeru Daily|last = Saeed|first = Ahmed|language = Divehi}}</ref> One of the ATR aircraft were brought to Male' in early 2007, and operations began to [[Gan International Airport|Gan]] in August.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.haveeru.com.mv/dhivehi/news/49706|script-title=dv:އެތެރޭގެ އުދުހުންތަކަށް ގެނައި ޓީއެމްއޭގެ ބޯޓް ޓެސްޓް ދަތުރެއްގައި ގަމަށް ޖައްސައިފި|date = 21 July 2007|accessdate = 11 December 2014|website = Haveeru Online|last = |first = |language = Divehi}}</ref> In 2009, TMA announced that they were suspending all domestic operations due to losses, and the two ATR aircraft acquired were subsequently sold.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.haveeru.com.mv/dhivehi/business/73956|title = TMA to sell two aircraft used for domestic aircraft. (Dhivehi)|date = 3 March 2009|accessdate = 11 December 2014|website = Haveeru Online|publisher = |last = |first = |language = Divehi}}</ref>

2011 saw the first [[De Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter|Twin Otter Series 400]] aircraft brought to the TMA fleet, bringing the total fleet to 23.

=== Merger with Maldivian Air Taxi ===
{{Main|Maldivian Air Taxi}}

[[Maldivian Air Taxi]], established in 1993, was the sole competitor of TMA in the seaplane transfer industry. The company boasted the world's largest seaplane fleet. On February 4, 2013, the American equity fund [[Blackstone Group]] announced their buyout of the majority stake of both Trans Maldivian Airways and [[Maldivian Air Taxi]], to form a new company with a combined fleet of 44 seaplanes, making it the largest seaplane fleet in the world by far.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url = http://www.haveeru.com.mv/news/47231|title = Blackstone buys majority stake in two Maldives seaplane operators|date = 4 February 2013|accessdate = |website = Haveeru Online|publisher = |last = |first = }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url = http://minivannews.com/politics/us-private-equity-fund-buys-both-maldivian-seaplane-operators-for-undisclosed-sum-52375|title = US private equity fund buys both Maldivian seaplane operators for undisclosed sum.|date = 4 February 2014|accessdate = |website = Minivan News|last = |first = }}</ref> The new company would retain the Trans Maldivian Airways brand name, with a new logo and livery integrating the colours of Maldivian Air Taxi.

The new merger company, with conjunction with the Maldives Transport Authority, has proposed to launch seaplane services to inhabited islands in the atolls, in addition to the currently served resort islands.<ref name=":0" />

=== Sold to Bain Capital ===
On December 18, 2017 &ndash; [[Bain Capital]] Private Equity announced that it has formed a consortium to acquire Trans Maldivian Airways ("TMA"), the world’s largest seaplane operator headquartered in the Maldives, from [[The Blackstone Group|Blackstone Group]]. The Bain Capital-led consortium includes Shenzhen Tempus Global Business Services Holdings Ltd ("Tempus Global") (300178) and TBRJ Fund 1 L.P. Bain Capital, which is headquartered in the US, will own approximately 80% of TMA.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.baincapital.com/news/bain-capital-private-equity-led-consortium-acquires-trans-maldivian-airways|title=Bain Capital Private Equity-led consortium acquires Trans Maldivian Airways|last=Capital|first=Bain|website=Bain Capital|access-date=2019-06-14}}</ref>

